    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d164">

                <pb facs="0323" n="151" sameAs="frus:frus1917Supp01v01/pg151"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 841.857/370</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Liverpool</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Washington</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Liverpool</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1917-02-27">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 27, 1917</hi></date>. <lb/> [<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Received 8.30 a.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Cunard Company state their steamer <hi rendition="#italic">Laconia</hi> torpedoed
                    without warning.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d164-f1"/> Six passengers and fourteen of
                    crew were Americans. Two American passengers, Mrs. Mary Hoy
                    and Elizabeth Hoy, died of exposure in boat which landed at
                    Bantry Bay, Ireland. Four other passengers died or are unaccounted for, and six
                    of the crew, nationality so far unknown, died. Other survivors landed
                    Queenstown.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d164-f1" n="3" xml:id="d164-f1-content">
                <p>Affidavits concerning this case are printed in European War No. 4: <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Diplomatic Correspondence with Belligerent Governments’
                        Relating to Neutral Rights and Duties</hi> (Washington, Government Printing
                    Office, 1918), pp. 282–291, Reprinted in Special Supplement to the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">American Journal of International Law</hi>, vol. 11
                    (1917), pp. 134–144.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
